10:10:23 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: Ancient cities are usually not found with every building perfectly intact. What survives depends on the building material, how it was buried, climate, later disturbance, and the kind of deposit covering it. A few reasons brick structures can survive underground: - **Burial is often gradual, not a crushing event.** Cities may accumulate layers from wind-blown dust, river silt, flood deposits, collapsed roofs and walls, trash, rebuilding, and later construction. This material settles around and inside structures rather than falling as one enormous destructive weight. - **The load is spread out.** Soil and rubble exert pressure, but walls—especially thick mudbrick or fired-brick walls—can support considerable vertical weight if they remain relatively dry and evenly supported. A buried wall is often reinforced laterally by the surrounding soil. - **Many “intact” buildings are only partly intact.** Archaeologists often uncover foundations, lower walls, floors, ovens, drains, door thresholds, and rooms preserved to a certain height. Upper stories, wooden roofs, plaster, and exposed wall tops commonly collapsed or decayed long ago. - **Mudbrick can survive surprisingly well in dry conditions.** In arid regions, dried mudbrick may remain stable for thousands of years once buried and protected from rain, erosion, sunlight, and temperature changes. Fired brick is even more durable. - **Burial can be protective.** Above ground, walls are attacked by weather, vegetation, scavenging for building stone, earthquakes, and people. Once covered, they are shielded from many of those processes. Pompeii, for example, was damaged by eruption debris but also preserved because volcanic ash sealed it rapidly. - **Some ancient cities were rebuilt repeatedly in the same place.** In Near Eastern “tells” or mounds, residents commonly built new structures on top of older ruined ones. Old walls might be filled in, leveled, reused as foundations, or incorporated into new construction. Over centuries this created a layered hill of occupation. Excavation is therefore careful and slow. Archaeologists remove soil and debris layer by layer, recording where each layer, wall, floor, artifact, and feature belongs. They do not simply dig down to a whole city at once; they reconstruct the sequence of occupation from those layers. So yes, many structures were damaged or demolished by collapse and later building. What archaeologists call “intact” generally means sufficiently preserved to identify rooms, walls, streets, and building phases—not necessarily standing exactly as they did when the city was inhabited.
