10:11:11 | INFO | 🤖 Target: The book-and-cardboard analogy is valid for some structures—especially roofs, timber frames, thin walls, and already-damaged buildings. Those commonly do collapse. But it does not work exactly like buried masonry because: - **Cardboard weakens with time and moisture** and bends under a concentrated load. Brick or stone masonry is much stronger in compression and does not “get tired” merely from a constant load in the same way. - **Dirt is not a stack of rigid books.** It settles, compacts, and spreads its load sideways. Once walls and rooms are filled with soil and rubble, the material inside and outside supports the walls. - **The pressure does not keep increasing because time passes.** It increases only if more material accumulates. A wall under 5 meters of soil experiences roughly the same long-term pressure whether it has been there 10 years or 2,000 years, aside from changes caused by water, ground movement, etc. - **Most ancient walls are thick and short.** A 1-meter-thick brick wall, often on a broad foundation, can carry far more vertical load than a cardboard box can. The usual sequence is more like this: 1. An abandoned building loses its roof—often because wood rots, burns, or is removed. 2. Upper walls may fall inward or erode. 3. The room fills with its own collapse debris. 4. Windblown soil, flood silt, rubbish, and later construction add more fill. 5. The remaining lower walls are packed and braced by the surrounding fill. That does not leave a complete standing house. It often leaves a room outline with lower walls, perhaps 0.5–2 meters high, buried floors, and features such as hearths or drains. In especially dry places or under rapid protective burial, more can survive; in wet climates or unstable ground, much less does. So your basic intuition is right that accumulated material contributes to destruction and that most ancient buildings become ruins. The correction is only that “buried for thousands of years” does not au