10:11:25 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That may well be what the documentary said, and it can be correct for the particular city or buildings it was describing. Heavy collapse debris and later deposits absolutely can destroy structures—especially roofs, wooden elements, thin walls, arches, already-cracked masonry, and buildings weakened by fire, earthquakes, flooding, or abandonment. A room whose roof and upper walls fall inward can be smashed and distorted by tons of rubble. Repeated rebuilding can add further damage. The nuance is that archaeologists distinguish between: - **collapse load:** a roof, upper story, wall, or earthquake debris falling onto a structure, often suddenly and unevenly; very destructive; - **overburden:** soil, silt, ash, rubbish, and rubble resting above a site after it is filled/buried; this can damage remains too, but it often does not compress every thick lower wall into a flat layer. A documentary narrator may reasonably summarize both as “the weight of debris crushed the city.” That is a useful plain-language description, even if the detailed archaeological reality is mixed: some portions were crushed, some collapsed first and were then buried, and some lower walls or foundations survived because the debris packed around them. So the correct overall picture is: - Most buildings were indeed wrecked over time. - Heavy debris was often one of the reasons. - Yet destruction is rarely total and uniform; enough robust, protected lower structure can remain for archaeologists to excavate. There is no contradiction between “the city was crushed by debris” and “some brick walls, floors, drains, or rooms survived underground.”
